## 1. Core MLE Responsibilities: Model Development Lifecycle

### 1.1 Day-to-Day Responsibilities (Synthesized from Internal Role Definitions)

Based on extensive internal role documentation across P30–P80 levels, the MLE role encompasses the full ML lifecycle:

**Problem Formulation & Data Exploration**
- Formulate ML problems end-to-end from business requirements
- Identify feasibility and usability validation techniques based on PM requirements
- Quantify business problems into ML-solvable formulations
- Explore and curate datasets; perform data quality assessment
- Collaborate with data engineering on upstream data contracts and schemas

**Feature Engineering**
- Develop robust feature engineering practices to ingest, process, and serve features for offline training and online inference at scale
- Design feature generation data pipelines (writing/updating feature values on daily/hourly basis)
- Build feature logging pipelines to construct ML training datasets (avoiding offline-online skew)
- Manage feature lifecycle: generation → storage → hydration → logging → training → serving

**Model Development & Training**
- Design system and model architectures
- Train sophisticated models (classical ML, deep learning, LLMs)
- Conduct rigorous experimentation and model evaluations
- Perform hyperparameter tuning and optimization
- Implement distributed training workflows on GPU clusters (PyTorch DDP, torchrun, Ray)
- Fine-tune pre-trained models (PEFT, LoRA) and open-source LLMs

**Evaluation & Validation**
- Continuously curate and collate validation datasets
- Perform periodical quantitative validation studies
- Design evaluation frameworks (bias-variance diagnostics, MRR, NDCG, xDCG metrics)
- Implement LLM-as-a-judge evaluation patterns
- Run offline experiment tracking with MLflow (parameters, metrics, artifacts)

**Deployment & Serving**
- Deploy models to production serving infrastructure (SageMaker, TEAMServe)
- Manage model lifecycle independent of application lifecycle
- Define deployment configurations and CI/CD for ML models
- Oversee model registration, versioning, and staging

**Monitoring & Optimization**
- Track model performance via analytics events and dashboards
- Implement drift detection (model, data, concept)
- Optimize latency, throughput, and resource efficiency
- Manage model retraining flows and continuous improvement

**Experimentation & A/B Testing**
- Design controlled experiments in production
- Implement A/B testing frameworks for model variants
- Run prompt optimization loops (GEPA-style for LLM applications)
- Track experiment results with statistical analysis

**Technical Debt Management**
- Lead code reviews and documentation
- Maintain ML pipeline health across feature, pipeline, and cost dimensions
- Contribute to improvements in ML development workflows
- Build and maintain reusable ML modules

### 1.2 AI Employee MLE Differentiation

For an **AI MLE employee**, key operational differences from human equivalents:

| Dimension | Human MLE | AI MLE Employee |
|-----------|-----------|-----------------|
| **Experimentation velocity** | Runs5–10 experiments/week manually | Runs 200+ experiment variants autonomously (per Symphony vision) |
| **Hypothesis generation** | Relies on intuition + literature | Systematically explores configuration space |
| **Code generation** | Writes code from scratch | Generates PR drafts, tests, documentation automatically |
| **Monitoring** | Periodic manual checks | Continuous 24/7 pipeline monitoring |
| **Escalation** | Self-determines when to ask for help | Must have explicit guardrails: confidence thresholds, human gates, default-to-escalate |
| **Judgment** | Makes architectural decisions autonomously | Proposes decisions, waits for human approval at gates |
| **Context retention** | Ramp time needed | Immediately grounded via context packs (codebase, architecture, incident history) |

**Key guardrails for AI MLE autonomy** (from internal Agentic Operations research):
- All autonomous fixes must be staged, not applied, until confidence threshold met or human approval given
- Default-to-escalate: any task not matching a known policy pattern escalates to human
- Every produced artifact goes through human gate before shipping
- Policies versioned alongside specifications; governance rotation ensures active review

