## 6. Team Structure and Organizational Fit

### 6.1 Reporting Relationships

Per [AI Employee Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6742955831) and [Digital MLE Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727):

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   Human Engineering Lead                  │
│              (Sets policy, reviews outcomes)              │
├─────────────────┬───────────────────┬───────────────────┤
│  Human MLE TL   │   Human MLE TL    │  Human Platform│
│  (IC6/IC7)      │   (IC6/IC7)       │  Lead│
│  Model design│   Research &      │  Infrastructure   │
│  & strategy     │   architecture│  decisions        │
├─────────────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────┤
│                │                   │                   │
│  ┌───────────┐  │                   │                   │
│  │ AI MLE    │  │  ┌───────────┐    │  ┌───────────┐    │
│  │ Employee  │  │  │ AI SRE    │    │  │ AI TPM    │    │
│  │ (IC5/IC6  │  │  │ Employee  │    │  │ Employee  │    │
│  │ execution)│  │  │ (Peer)│    │  │ (Peer)    │    │
│  └───────────┘  │  └───────────┘    │  └───────────┘    │
└─────────────────┴───────────────────┴───────────────────┘
```

**Key Principle**: The Digital MLE covers the **execution and instrumentation surface area** of the IC5/IC6 profile — freeing human MLEs to operate at the IC6/IC7 layer of model design, research, and architectural strategy ([Digital MLE Vision](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/7051610727)).

### 6.2 Relationship to Other AI Employees

Per [AI Employee Strategy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6742955831):

| **AI Employee** | **Owns** | **Relationship to AI MLE** |
|----------------|---------|---------------------------|
| **Digital SRE** | Service & codebase reliability | Peer — handles infra incidents; AI MLE handles model incidents |
| **Digital TPM** | Delivery board & coordination | Peer — coordinates timelines; AI MLE reports on experiment progress |
| **Digital Manager** | Team coordination surface | Oversight — routes work to AI MLE; monitors output quality |
| **Digital SWE** | Codebase & service code | Peer — AI MLE owns ML code; SWE owns application code |

### 6.3 Decision-Making Authority Boundaries

Per [RFC-002AI SDLC Governance Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AISDLC/pages/6849219616):

| **Decision Type** | **Authority** | **Rationale** |
|------------------|--------------|---------------|
| Which experiments to run | 🟢 AI MLE autonomous | Low risk, reversible |
| Hyperparameter selection | 🟢 AI MLE autonomous | Within established bounds |
| Feature engineering choices | 🟡Propose to human | May have data governance implications |
| Model architecture selection | 🔴 Human MLE/TL | Strategic impact, hard to reverse |
| Production deployment | 🔴 Human approval required | Customer impact |
| Compute budget allocation | 🟡 Within pre-approved limits | Financial impact |
| Data source selection | 🟡Requires governance review | Privacy/compliance |
| Retraining trigger response | 🟡 AI MLE diagnoses, human decides scope | Risk of cascading issues |
| Emergency model takedown | 🟢 If pre-approved criteria met | Customer protection |
| Cross-team resource requests | 🟡 AI MLE proposes, human negotiates | Organizational politics |

### 6.4 Agent Creation and Subordinate Management

Per [Autonomy Model and Safety Controls](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ALTA/pages/6765976100):

- AI MLE **may** create subordinate agents under itself (e.g., specialized evaluation agents, monitoring agents)
- AI MLE **may** retire/suspend/reconfigure its own subordinate agents within policy
- AI MLE **may not** create or mutate agents outside its own tree
- AI MLE **may not** take control of other agent trees (including SRE Agent's tree)
- All agent creation is bounded by: allowed types, max depth, max breadth, resource budgets, review requirements

### 6.5 Maturity Progression

Per [AI SDLC Maturity Levels](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AISDLC/pages/6849219616):

| **Level** | **AI MLE Capability** | **Human Role** |
|----------|----------------------|----------------|
| L1 (Assistive) | Suggests experiment configs, drafts analysis | Human reviews all outputs |
| L2 (Structured) | Performs bounded tasks with workflow integration | Human approves every action |
| L3 (Conditional Autonomy) | Executes low-risk tasks autonomously (monitoring, alerting, experiment execution) | Human oversees exceptions |
| **L4 (High Autonomy) — TARGET** | Handles most in-scope tasks autonomously (full experiment lifecycle, retraining triggers) | Human governs policy and incident response |
| L5 (Near-full Autonomy) | Primary decision-maker for eligible ML work | Human provides governance and emergency intervention |

**Initial deployment**: L2–L3, progressing to L4 as trust builds (measured by Time-to-Delegation)

### 6.6 Communication and Reporting Cadence

| **Communication** | **Frequency** | **Medium** | **Content** |
|------------------|--------------|-----------|-------------|
| Status update | Daily | Slack (automated) | Experiments completed, metrics, blockers |
| Escalation request | Event-driven | Slack (structured) + Jira ticket | Context, proposed action, approval needed |
| Experiment report | Per experiment completion | Confluence (auto-generated) | Results, analysis, recommendation |
| Weekly summary | Weekly | Confluence page | Key metrics trends, accomplishments, risks |
| Reasoning trace | Per significant decision | Audit log | Full reasoning chain for review |
| Incident report | Per incident | Confluence + Jira | Root cause, timeline, mitigation, prevention |

